## Section 7: Abstract Classes

### 1. Learning goal

Use abstract classes to define shared code plus required methods for subclasses.

### 2. Simple explanation

An abstract class is a class you cannot instantiate directly.

It can contain:

- Normal properties
- Normal methods with implementation
- Abstract methods with no implementation

Subclasses must implement abstract methods.

### 3. Code example

```ts
abstract class PaymentMethod {
  constructor(protected amount: number) {}

  printReceipt(): void {
    console.log(`Paid $${this.amount}`);
  }

  abstract pay(): void;
}

class CreditCardPayment extends PaymentMethod {
  constructor(amount: number, private cardLast4: string) {
    super(amount);
  }

  override pay(): void {
    console.log(`Charging card ending in ${this.cardLast4}`);
  }
}

const payment = new CreditCardPayment(50, "4242");

payment.pay();
payment.printReceipt();

// Not allowed:
// const basePayment = new PaymentMethod(50);
```

Expected output:

```txt
Charging card ending in 4242
Paid $50
```

### 4. Raw intuition

An abstract class says:

"Every subclass is this kind of thing, and I can share some real code, but each subclass must fill in specific blanks."

It is a partial blueprint.

### 5. Common mistakes

- Trying to instantiate an abstract class.
- Creating an abstract class with no shared code when an interface would be enough.
- Forgetting to implement abstract methods in subclasses.
- Making too many abstract methods too early.
- Using abstract classes to force a hierarchy that does not really exist.

