## Section 13: Dependency Injection Basics

### 1. Learning goal

Use constructor dependency injection to give an object its collaborators from the outside.

### 2. Simple explanation

Dependency injection means an object receives the things it needs instead of creating them internally.

A dependency is another object/class/function your class needs to do its job.

Constructor injection is the simplest form:

```ts
class Service {
  constructor(private dependency: Dependency) {}
}
```

### 3. Code example

Without dependency injection:

```ts
class ConsoleLogger {
  log(message: string): void {
    console.log(message);
  }
}

class OrderService {
  private logger = new ConsoleLogger();

  placeOrder(): void {
    this.logger.log("Order placed");
  }
}
```

With dependency injection:

```ts
interface Logger {
  log(message: string): void;
}

class ConsoleLogger implements Logger {
  log(message: string): void {
    console.log(message);
  }
}

class OrderService {
  constructor(private logger: Logger) {}

  placeOrder(): void {
    this.logger.log("Order placed");
  }
}

const logger = new ConsoleLogger();
const service = new OrderService(logger);

service.placeOrder();
```

Expected output:

```txt
Order placed
```

### 4. Raw intuition

Without dependency injection:

"I build my own tools."

With dependency injection:

"Someone hands me the tools I need."

That makes the class easier to test and easier to change.

### 5. Common mistakes

- Thinking dependency injection requires a framework.
- Injecting too many things because the class has too many responsibilities.
- Depending on concrete classes when an interface would be better.
- Creating dependencies inside the class and calling it injection.
- Making everything injectable even when there is no real variation or testing need.

### 6. Exercises

1. Create an interface `PaymentGateway` with `charge(amount: number): boolean`.
