## Section 15: Final Project and Final Exam

### 1. Learning goal

Build a small TypeScript OOP application that uses every core concept in this course.

### 2. Simple explanation

The final project is a tiny task management system.

It must include:

- Class
- Object
- Constructor
- Properties
- Methods
- Interface
- `implements`
- Abstract class
- Inheritance
- Method overriding
- Composition
- Polymorphism
- `private`, `public`, and `protected`
- Static property or method
- Dependency injection

You are not building a full app UI. You are building the domain model and running it from a script.

### 3. Code example

This is a small worked example of the style you will use. Do not copy it as the final project.

```ts
interface Notifier {
  send(message: string): void;
}

class ConsoleNotifier implements Notifier {
  send(message: string): void {
    console.log(message);
  }
}

abstract class Task {
  private static nextId = 1;
  public readonly id: number;

  constructor(public title: string, protected notifier: Notifier) {
    this.id = Task.nextId;
    Task.nextId += 1;
  }

  complete(): void {
    this.notifier.send(`Completed: ${this.title}`);
  }

  abstract estimateHours(): number;
}

class BugFixTask extends Task {
  constructor(title: string, notifier: Notifier, private severity: "low" | "high") {
    super(title, notifier);
  }

  override estimateHours(): number {
    return this.severity === "high" ? 6 : 2;
  }
}

const task = new BugFixTask("Fix login bug", new ConsoleNotifier(), "high");

console.log(task.id);
console.log(task.estimateHours());
task.complete();
```

Expected output:

```txt
1
6
Completed: Fix login bug
```

### 4. Raw intuition

The project should feel like objects collaborating:

- A task knows its own title and completion rules.
- A task list manages tasks.
- A notifier sends messages.
- Different task types estimate work differently.
- The app code can treat all task types as `Task`.

You are practicing object boundaries, not just syntax.

### 5. Common mistakes

- Making one giant `TaskManager` that does everything.
- Using inheritance where composition is better.
- Creating interfaces that are never used.
- Making all properties public.
- Avoiding polymorphism by writing many `if (task.type === "...")` checks.
- Forgetting to use `override`.
- Creating dependencies inside classes instead of injecting them.

